DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
"PGstandard.pl",
"MathObjects.pl",
"parserPopUp.pl",
"PGgraders.pl",
);

TEXT(beginproblem());

Context("Numeric");
# 7.2
$popup2 = PopUp(
  ["?","Parametric form","Implicit form","Explicit form"],
  "Implicit form",
);
# 7.4
$popup4 = PopUp(
  ["?","Parametric form","Implicit form","Explicit form"],
  "Parametric form",
);
# 7.5
$popup5 = PopUp(
  ["?","Parametric form","Implicit form","Explicit form"],
  "Explicit form",
);
# 7.6
$popup6 = PopUp(
  ["?","Parametric form","Implicit form","Explicit form"],
  "Implicit form",
);

Context()->texStrings;
BEGIN_TEXT
The concept of "dimension" will be a key hurdle to defining manifolds. Right now, you can build some intuition by borrowing from linear algebra. There are 3 heuristic principles:
$BR
\(\qquad\) 1. If a set can be described by \(n\) "independent" variables,	then the set should presumably be a \(n\)-dimensional manifold. 
$BR
\(\qquad\) 2. If a set can be described by \(m-n\) "dependent" variables and \(n\) "independent" variables, then the set should presumably be a  \(n\)-dimensional manifold.
$BR
\(\qquad\) 3. If a set can be described by \(m\) variables and \(n\) "independent" equations, then the set should presumably be a \((m-n)\)-dimensional manifold.
$BR
$BR
Let \(m,n\in\mathbb{N}^+\) with \(n<m\). For each set \(S\) below, identify whether it is written in parametric, explicit, or implicit form. Assuming each set is a manifold, guess its "dimension" using a heuristic principle. Write the dimension in the form of number, e.g. type a if the set is a-dimensional
$BR
$BR
(b) \(S=\lbrace (x_1,\dots,x_n) \in \mathbb{R}^n : x_1^2 + x_2^2 + \cdots + x_n^2 = 1\rbrace \) dim=\{ans_rule(5)\} \{ $popup2->menu() \} 
END_TEXT
ANS(str_cmp("n-1"));
ANS( $popup2->cmp() );
BEGIN_TEXT
$BR
(c) There exists \(U\subseteq\mathbb{R}^n\) and continuous \(f:U\to\mathbb{R}^m\) such that
$BCENTER
\(S = \lbrace f(x_1,x_2,\dots,x_n) : (x_1,\dots,x_n) \in U \rbrace = f(U)\)
$ECENTER
dim=\{ans_rule(5)\} \{ $popup4->menu() \} 
END_TEXT
ANS(str_cmp("n"));
ANS( $popup4->cmp() );
BEGIN_TEXT
$BR
(d) There exists \(V\subseteq\mathbb{R}^n\) and continuous \(h: V\to\mathbb{R}^{m-n}\) such that
$BCENTER
\(S = \lbrace (x_1,x_2,\dots,x_n,x_{n+1},\dots,x_m) \in \mathbb{R}^m : (x_{n+1},\dots,x_m) = h(x_1,\dots,x_n), (x_1,\dots,x_n)\in V \rbrace \)
$ECENTER
dim=\{ans_rule(5)\} \{ $popup5->menu() \} 
END_TEXT
ANS(str_cmp("n"));
ANS( $popup5->cmp() );
BEGIN_TEXT
$BR
(e) There exists \(W\subseteq\mathbb{R}^n\) and continuous \(h: V\to\mathbb{R}^{m-n}\) such that
$BCENTER
\(S =  \lbrace (x_1,x_2,\dots,x_n,x_{n+1},\dots,x_m) \in W \times \mathbb{R}^{m-n} :  g(x_1,\dots,x_n) = \underbrace{(0,0,\dots,0)}_{m-n \text{ times}} \rbrace\)
$ECENTER
dim=\{ans_rule(5)\} \{ $popup6->menu() \} 
END_TEXT
ANS(str_cmp("n"));
ANS( $popup6->cmp() );

Context()->normalStrings;

install_problem_grader(~~&custom_problem_grader_fluid);
